]{name=methodology, column=3, between=row5 and row10}{
  \colorbox{warningbg}{\parbox{0.91\linewidth}{%
    \fontsize{19}{25}\selectfont\color{amber2}
    \textbf{Cross-version comparisons are unreliable.}\\[3pt]
    \color{textprimary}
    The benchmark explicitly warns: \textit{"Comparisons across Chrome versions aren't always safe since the benchmark and its driver are constantly developing."}
  }}\vspace{7pt}

  \fontsize{19}{26}\selectfont\color{textprimary}
  \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.5}
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
  \begin{tabular}{p{0.17\linewidth}p{0.10\linewidth}p{0.60\linewidth}}
    \rowcolor{panelbg2}
    \textcolor{amber}{\bfseries Chrome} &
    \textcolor{amber}{\bfseries Year} &
    \textcolor{amber}{\bfseries Change} \\
    \rowcolor{rowdark}
    \textcolor{amber}{\bfseries 118} & 2023 &
    \textcolor{textprimary}{Weighted geometric mean replaces arithmetic} \\
    \rowcolor{rowlight}
    118 & 2023 &
    \textcolor{textprimary}{CPU throttling recalibrated} \\
    \rowcolor{rowdark}
    118+ & 2023+ &
    \textcolor{textprimary}{Timing \& driver instrumentation refined} \\
    \rowcolor{rowlight}
    139+ & 2025+ &
    \textcolor{textprimary}{Odd versions: keyed-only benchmarks} \\
  \end{tabular}
  \vspace{6pt}

  \colorbox{panelbg2}{\parbox{0.91\linewidth}{%
    \fontsize{18}{24}\selectfont
    \textcolor{amber}{\bfseries $\Rightarrow$}\ Before Chrome 118: arithmetic mean.\
    After: weighted geo. mean.\
    \textcolor{amber}{\bfseries Scores are not comparable across this boundary.}
  }}
}

%% — Why Chrome 118 Is the Dividing Line (rows 10–14) —
\posterbox[ambercard, title={\fontsize{28}{34}\selectfont\bfseries\color{amber} Why Chrome 118 Is the Dividing Line}
]{name=chrome118, column=3, between=row10 and row14}{
  \fontsize{20}{27}\selectfont\color{textprimary}

  \colorbox{panelbg2}{\parbox{0.91\linewidth}{%
    \fontsize{18}{24}\selectfont\color{textsecondary}
    \textcolor{amber}{\bfseries Before Chrome 118:}\\
    \textcolor{textprimary}{Arithmetic mean of individual operation scores}\\[4pt]
    \textcolor{amber}{\bfseries After Chrome 118:}\\
    \textcolor{textprimary}{Weighted geometric mean — penalizes extreme outliers more fairly}
  }}\vspace{6pt}

  \fontsize{19}{25}\selectfont\color{textprimary}
  \begin{itemize}[itemsep=4pt, topsep=2pt]
    \item Geometric mean reduces sensitivity to single slow operations
    \item CPU throttle factors were recalibrated simultaneously
    \item A "better" score in Chrome 119 vs Chrome 117 may just reflect the new formula
    \item \textcolor{amber}{\bfseries Always check which Chrome version you're comparing}
  \end{itemize}
}

%% — Keyed vs Non-Keyed (rows 14–17) —
\posterbox[bluecard, title={\fontsize{28}{34}\selectfont\bfseries\color{electricblue} Keyed vs Non-Keyed Modes}
]{name=keyed, column=3, between=row14 and row17}{
  \fontsize{20}{27}\selectfont\color{textprimary}

  \colorbox{panelbg2}{\parbox{0.91\linewidth}{%
    \fontsize{18}{24}\selectfont
    \textcolor{teal}{\bfseries Keyed:}\ Framework manages its own identity per row (React, Angular, Vue...)\\[3pt]
    \textcolor{electricblue}{\bfseries Non-Keyed:}\ Row elements can be recycled freely (Svelte, Solid, Inferno...)
  }}\vspace{5pt}

  \colorbox{warningbg}{\parbox{0.91\linewidth}{%
    \fontsize{18}{24}\selectfont\color{amber2}
    \textbf{Chrome 139+: Odd versions} are keyed-only runs.\\
    Always check the run's mode before comparing frameworks.
  }}\vspace{4pt}

  \fontsize{18}{24}\selectfont\color{textsecondary}
  Non-keyed frameworks often score faster — but they solve a different problem. Keyed vs non-keyed results are \textbf{not directly comparable.}
}

